href="/about/">About</a></nav></div></header><main id="main"><header class="article-hero"><div class="container"><nav class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/food-crisis-by-country/">Food crisis by country</a><span>›</span><span>India Food Inflation 2026: Latest CPI Data & Trends</span></nav><div class="article-hero-grid"><div><span class="article-category">Country briefing</span><h1 class="article-title">India Food Inflation 2026: Latest CPI Data & Trends</h1><p class="article-dek">India's official data show food inflation at 7.74% year over year in September 2026. That is evidence of price pressure—not, by itself, proof of a nationwide physical food shortage.</p><div class="byline"><span><a href="/about/">World Food Crisis Editorial Desk</a></span><span>Updated 17 October 2026</span></div><figure class="article-featured-image"><img src="/images/articles/india-food-inflation.jpg" alt="Food staples in an Indian market, illustrating the effect of food-price inflation on households." loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></figure></div><aside class="article-hero-side"><span class="side-label">The key takeaway</span><strong>India's food-inflation rate measures price change in a defined consumer basket. It is not the same as food availability, a household affordability measure or an acute food-insecurity classification.</strong><p>Educational information, not an emergency alert.</p></aside></div></div></header><div class="container article-shell"><article class="article-content"><p class="lede">India food inflation in 2026 should be assessed using the dated Consumer Price Index release and its food index, not headline CPI alone. Monthly national figures describe average price change; they do not mean every food item or household faces the same costs.</p>
<div class="answer-box"><strong>Latest official headline figures</strong><ul><li><strong>Food inflation:</strong> 7.74% year over year, September 2026 (provisional).</li><li><strong>Rural food inflation:</strong> 8.06% year over year.</li><li><strong>Urban food inflation:</strong> 7.18% year over year.</li><li><strong>Overall CPI inflation:</strong> 5.54% year over year.</li></ul><p>Source: India's Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation, CPI release published 12 October 2026.</p></div>
<h2>What the September 2026 number actually means</h2>
<p>The Consumer Food Price Index (CFPI) measures changes in prices for a defined food basket, comparing September 2026 with September 2025. A 7.74% year-on-year rate means that the measured basket was 7.74% higher on average than in the corresponding month a year earlier. It is not a claim that all individual items, states or households experienced the same increase. The September figures are provisional and can be revised.</p>
<p>The same release put all-India headline CPI inflation at 5.54%. Food inflation was therefore higher than the headline rate. Rural food inflation, at 8.06%, also exceeded the urban rate of 7.18% in the reported figures. Those differences are useful signals for monitoring household cost pressure, but they do not alone explain why it happened. Product-level changes, weights in the CPI basket, local supply, incomes and household spending patterns all matter.</p>
<p>Read the <a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2322244&lang=1&reg=3">official September CPI release</a> before quoting the number. It includes the reference period, rural and urban breakdowns, index levels and provisional status.</p>
<h2>Which food items are moving the most?</h2>
<p>The same September release shows why a national average can hide very different shopping experiences. The Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation reported the following year-on-year item inflation rates at the all-India combined level. These are percentage changes compared with September 2025—not month-on-month price changes and not prices in every local market.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Food item</th><th>Year-on-year inflation, August 2026</th><th>Year-on-year inflation, September 2026</th><th>How to read the change</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Onion</td><td>48.78%</td><td>108.09%</td><td>A sharp acceleration in the reported annual rate; local prices and seasonal arrivals still need checking.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ginger</td><td>73.82%</td><td>62.85%</td><td>Inflation remained high but eased compared with August's annual rate.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Garlic</td><td>43.60%</td><td>54.62%</td><td>The annual rate rose; the national figure does not show each state's retail price.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lemon</td><td>15.15%</td><td>36.41%</td><td>The year-on-year rate accelerated in the published item data.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Potato</td><td>−13.15%</td><td>−11.50%</td><td>Prices were below the year-earlier level even though the deflation rate narrowed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tomato</td><td>−31.09%</td><td>−8.62%</td><td>Prices remained below the year-earlier level, but the annual decline became smaller.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>This is a more useful way to investigate a household budget: identify the foods your household buys most, compare local prices over time, and note whether a change is a national annual rate or the actual price at a nearby market. These figures do not justify assuming every food category is rising at the same rate.</p>
<p>Source: <a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2322244&lang=1&reg=3">MoSPI's September 2026 CPI release</a>, published 12 October 2026. The headline figures are provisional; check the original release for basket weights, rural/urban splits and future revisions.</p>
<h2>Does rising food inflation mean India is running out of food?</h2>
<p>No. Inflation measures price change, not the physical quantity of food available in every market. Prices can rise when one or more crops face weather-related pressure, transport or input costs increase, demand changes, or the cost of moving food from farms and wholesale markets to shops shifts. Some products can become more expensive while other products remain stable or become cheaper.</p>
<p>Likewise, national production and stock figures do not tell the full household story. A country may have adequate aggregate supplies while particular regions experience temporary stockouts, expensive transport or limited purchasing power. The reverse is possible too: local prices may be stable in one period even though some households cannot afford a nutritious diet. Food prices, physical availability and food security are related indicators, but they answer different questions.</p>
<p>For a broader explanation, see our guides to <a href="/articles/food-prices-vs-shortages/">food prices and shortages</a> and <a href="/articles/food-security-vs-availability/">food availability versus food security</a>.</p>
<h2>Why the effect differs between households and regions</h2>
<p>A national inflation rate is an average across a basket and geographic areas. A household that spends a large share of its budget on food may feel a price rise more sharply than a higher-income household. People who grow some of their own food may be exposed to different costs and seasonal patterns from people who buy nearly everything in retail markets. A family also has a different experience depending on which foods it buys, its location, transport options, household size and dietary needs.</p>
<p>Rural and urban results should therefore be treated as useful summaries, not descriptions of every district or family. If the question concerns a particular city or state, national CPI should be paired with the most relevant state-level or market-level information available. A national figure should not be used to infer the price of one vegetable, a bag of rice or the entire shopping bill.</p>
<h2>Which indicators should readers check next?</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Monthly CPI and CFPI releases:</strong> compare year-on-year rates, month-to-month index movement where reported, rural and urban splits, and whether figures are provisional.</li>
<li><strong>Product-level prices:</strong> look for changes in the foods that matter most to the household or question being investigated.</li>
<li><strong>Wholesale and market arrivals:</strong> where reliable data are available, compare commodity prices and arrivals instead of relying on a national average alone.</li>
<li><strong>Supply-side context:</strong> read crop forecasts, weather updates, stocks and trade information for the commodity involved.</li>
<li><strong>Affordability and access:</strong> price data should be interpreted alongside income, livelihoods, transport and the presence of functioning markets.</li></ul>
<p>Each indicator has limits. Retail inflation shows price movement, crop forecasts describe expected production, and food-security assessments examine access and outcomes for defined populations and periods. Combining sources is more reliable than asking one number to answer every question.</p>
<h2>What readers can do with this information</h2>
<p>For household budgeting, track a small basket of foods you actually buy and compare unit prices over several shopping trips. Plan meals around familiar seasonal alternatives and reduce waste before buying extra quantities. Our <a href="/tools/food-budget-impact/">food-budget impact calculator</a> can illustrate a hypothetical percentage change; it is not a forecast of Indian grocery prices.</p>
<p>For a news claim, check the original release date and the period measured. Do not equate a national food-inflation statistic with a claim that a specific food is unavailable. For a local concern, check current local market information and advice from relevant public authorities.</p>
<h2>Sources and data notes</h2>
<ul><li><a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2322244&lang=1&reg=3">Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation: CPI for September 2026</a> (released 12 October 2026; provisional).</li><li><a href="https://www.fao.org/worldfoodsituation/FoodPricesIndex/en/">FAO Food Price Index</a> for international commodity-price context; it is not a direct measure of Indian retail inflation.</li><li><a href="https://www.fao.org/worldfoodsituation/csdb/en/">FAO Cereal Supply and Demand Brief</a> for global cereal production, use, trade and stock forecasts.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> the September CFPI reading points to meaningful food-price pressure at the national level. It does not prove that India has a single nationwide physical shortage. The location, product and household context are essential.</p>
<section class="article-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><h2 id="faq-title">Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What was India's food inflation in September 2026?</h3><p>The official provisional year-on-year Consumer Food Price Index inflation rate was 7.74%, according to the release published on 12 October 2026.</p><h3>Was food inflation higher in rural or urban India?</h3><p>The reported rate was 8.06% in rural areas and 7.18% in urban areas. These are broad averages, not the rate every household faced.</p><h3>Does food inflation mean food is physically unavailable?</h3><p>No. Inflation measures price changes. Availability needs other evidence, such as product-level stocks, market arrivals and local reports of persistent stockouts.</p><h3>Where can I find the original data?</h3><p>Use the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation's <a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2322244&lang=1&reg=3">September 2026 CPI release</a> and check the provisional status and reference dates.</p></section></article><aside class="article-aside"><div class="aside-card"><h3>Useful resources</h3><a href="/data/">Food-supply data desk</a><a href="/sources/">Primary sources</a><a href="/methodology/">Editorial methodology</a><a href="/tools/food-budget-impact/">Budget calculator</a></div></aside></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="container footer-bottom"><span>© <span data-year>2026</span> World Food Crisis</span><span>Sources over speculation.</span></div></footer></body></html>